course: dictionary
lesson: 14159
title: Mistranslations
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A translation changes words from one language into another. A mistranslation is a wrong translation, where the meaning has changed by mistake. «The shop sign said "Please touch the monkeys", but it should have said "Please do not touch the monkeys".» The word "not" was lost, so the meaning flipped.
explain: Idioms are phrases whose meaning is not in the single words. «Grandma said "it is raining cats and dogs", and the translation said that cats and dogs were falling from the sky.» This idiom means it is raining very hard, so translating word for word gives a silly result. This is why translating is hard.
explain: One word can also have more than one meaning, so a translator must pick the right one. Use the words around it, which is its context, to decide. Good translators read their work back to check that it still makes sense.
fact: A translation changes the words of one language into another language.
fact: A mistranslation is a wrong translation, where the meaning has changed by mistake.
fact: «A funny mistranslation» on a menu or sign can make people laugh, but it can also confuse them.
fact: Some famous stories about mistranslations are myths, so check the facts before you repeat them.
fact: «Lost in translation» means that some meaning is missed when words move from one language to another.
exercise: «The sign said "Please touch the monkeys" instead of "Please do not touch the monkeys".» What kind of mistake is this? | *A mistranslation, where the meaning changed by mistake | A kind of song | A long, slow sentence | A correct translation
exercise: Why can an idiom like «raining cats and dogs» be hard to translate word for word? | It is always a very short phrase | *Its meaning is not in the single words | It has no meaning in any language | Every word is a cat or a dog
exercise: A word has more than one meaning. What helps a translator choose the right one? | *The words and sentences around it, which is its context | Guessing without reading | Ignoring the sentence | The longest meaning in the dictionary
test: What does «lost in translation» mean? | A translator has lost their pen | Words are much longer in other languages | Something has been found in a dictionary | *Some meaning is missed when words move into another language
test: What is a good check after translating a sentence? | Add more adjectives everywhere | Copy the original word for word | Count the letters | *Read it back and see if it still makes sense
exam: Why is a funny mistranslation on a menu useful to a learner? | *It shows that words need careful checking before they are used | It proves that no two languages have any links at all | All translations are funny | Dictionaries are useless
